 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L06_001_Missing Colon",
   "display_name": "Missing Colon",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 6! So far you have been writing code. In this lab you will <b>fix</b> code. Every programmer, even an expert, writes code with mistakes. Finding and fixing them is called <b>debugging</b>, and it is one of the most useful skills you can learn.</p>\n<p>Mistakes in Python programs come in a few kinds:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Syntax errors:</b> the code breaks Python's grammar rules (a missing <code>:</code> or bracket, a misspelled keyword). Python refuses to run the program at all.</li><li><b>Runtime errors:</b> the grammar is fine, but something goes wrong while the program runs (using a name that does not exist, adding text to a number, reading past the end of a list). The program stops at that line.</li><li><b>Logical errors:</b> the program runs and finishes, but the answer is wrong. Python gives no message at all, so these are the hardest to find.</li></ul>\n<p><b>How to read an error message.</b> When Python finds an error, it prints a message like this one (for a program where line 3 is <code>if age &gt;= 18</code> with no colon):</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 3\n  if age &gt;= 18\n              ^\nSyntaxError: expected &#x27;:&#x27;</pre>\n<p>Read it from the bottom up:</p>\n<ul><li>The <b>last line</b> gives the error type (<code>SyntaxError</code>) and a short message (<code>expected ':'</code>).</li><li>The line above shows the <b>line number</b> and the code on that line, and <code>^</code> points near the problem.</li></ul>\n<p>(The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>The rule for this error:</b> every line that starts a block (<code>if</code>, <code>elif</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>def</code>) must end with a colon <code>:</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Pre-check and Check in this lab.</b> This lab has a small penalty: each failed <b>Check</b> lowers the marks that question can still earn. <b>Pre-check is always free.</b> So press Pre-check to see the error message, fix the code, Pre-check again, and press Check only when you think the code is correct.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a student's marks (an integer from 0 to 100) and should print <code>Pass</code> if the marks are 40 or more, otherwise <code>Fail</code>. It has a syntax error. Fix it.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nmarks = int(input())\nif marks >= 40\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "55",
     "expected": "Pass",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "40",
     "expected": "Pass",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "12",
     "expected": "Fail",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "39",
     "expected": "Fail",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "100",
     "expected": "Pass",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L06_002_Missing Closing Bracket",
   "display_name": "Missing Closing Bracket",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Brackets always come in pairs: every <code>(</code> needs a matching <code>)</code>, and every <code>[</code> needs a matching <code>]</code>. If one is missing, Python reaches the end of the line (or the program) still waiting for it, and reports a syntax error. For example:</p>\n<pre>x = 5\nprint(&quot;Value:&quot;, x</pre>\n<p>gives:</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  print(&quot;Value:&quot;, x\n       ^\nSyntaxError: &#x27;(&#x27; was never closed</pre>\n<p>Notice that the message says <b>which bracket</b> was never closed and on which line it was opened. Sometimes Python notices the problem only on a later line, so if the line it points to looks fine, look at the line <b>just before</b> it too. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a name and should print a greeting in exactly this format: <code>Hello, &lt;name&gt;!</code>. Fix the syntax error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>Asha</code>, the output is <code>Hello, Asha!</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nname = input()\nprint(\"Hello, \" + name + \"!\"\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Asha",
     "expected": "Hello, Asha!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Zoro",
     "expected": "Hello, Zoro!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Ravi Kumar",
     "expected": "Hello, Ravi Kumar!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Hermione",
     "expected": "Hello, Hermione!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L06_003_Unterminated String",
   "display_name": "Unterminated String",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>string</b> (text) must start and end with the same kind of quote: <code>\"like this\"</code> or <code>'like this'</code>. If the closing quote is missing, Python keeps reading everything after the opening quote as text, until the end of the line, and then reports an error:</p>\n<pre>city = &quot;Goa\nprint(city)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 1\n  city = &quot;Goa\n         ^\nSyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)</pre>\n<p>A related mistake is putting the closing quote in the wrong place, so that code which should be outside the string ends up inside it. Look carefully at where each string starts and ends. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads an integer <code>n</code> and should print the text <code>Double is:</code> followed by the value of <code>2 * n</code>, separated by a space. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>5</code>, the output is <code>Double is: 10</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>print(\"Total:\", t)</code> prints the text, a space, then the value of <code>t</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\nprint(\"Double is:, n * 2)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "Double is: 10",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "Double is: 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-7",
     "expected": "Double is: -14",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "21",
     "expected": "Double is: 42",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "1000",
     "expected": "Double is: 2000",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L06_004_Assignment Instead Of Comparison",
   "display_name": "Assignment Instead Of Comparison",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Python, <code>=</code> and <code>==</code> mean very different things:</p>\n<ul><li><code>x = 5</code> is <b>assignment</b>: store 5 in <code>x</code>.</li><li><code>x == 5</code> is <b>comparison</b>: is <code>x</code> equal to 5? The answer is <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>A condition in an <code>if</code> or <code>while</code> must be a comparison, so it needs <code>==</code>. Using <code>=</code> there is a syntax error:</p>\n<pre>count = 3\nif count = 3:\n    print(&quot;three&quot;)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  if count = 3:\n     ^^^^^^^^^\nSyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant &#x27;==&#x27; or &#x27;:=&#x27; instead of &#x27;=&#x27;?</pre>\n<p>Newer versions of Python even suggest the fix (\"Maybe you meant '=='...\"). (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads an integer and should print <code>Even</code> if it is even, otherwise <code>Odd</code>. (An integer is even when the remainder after dividing by 2 is 0; <code>0</code> is even, and negative integers work the same way.) Fix the error.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\nif n % 2 = 0:\n    print(\"Even\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Odd\")\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
