     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L06_005_Misspelled Keyword",
   "display_name": "Misspelled Keyword",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>Keywords</b> are the special words of Python: <code>if</code>, <code>elif</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>def</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>False</code> and a few more. They must be spelled exactly right, in lowercase. A misspelled keyword is just an unknown word to Python, and the line no longer makes sense:</p>\n<pre>count = 0\nwhlie count &lt; 3:\n    count = count + 1</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  whlie count &lt; 3:\n        ^^^^^\nSyntaxError: invalid syntax</pre>\n<p>A misspelled keyword usually gives the plain message <code>invalid syntax</code>, with <code>^</code> pointing at or just after the word. When you see <code>invalid syntax</code> and the line looks almost right, <b>check the spelling of every keyword on that line</b>. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a positive integer <code>n</code> (int &gt; 0) and should print the numbers from 1 to <code>n</code>, one per line. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>3</code>, the output is:</p>\n<pre>1\n2\n3</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\ni = 1\nwhlie i <= n:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
