    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L06_006_Else If Instead Of Elif",
   "display_name": "Else If Instead Of Elif",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Some other programming languages write <code>else if</code>. Python uses one word: <b><code>elif</code></b>. Writing <code>else if</code> is a syntax error, because after <code>else</code> Python expects only a colon:</p>\n<pre>if x &gt; 0:\n    print(&quot;positive&quot;)\nelse if x &lt; 0:\n    print(&quot;negative&quot;)</pre>\n<p>gives <code>SyntaxError</code> pointing at the <code>if</code> after <code>else</code> (the message is usually <code>expected ':'</code> or <code>invalid syntax</code>). (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p>Remember the shape of a chain of conditions:</p>\n<pre>if condition1:\n    ...\nelif condition2:\n    ...\nelse:\n    ...</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a temperature (an integer, in degrees Celsius) and should print:</p>\n<ul><li><code>Hot</code> if the temperature is more than 30,</li><li><code>Pleasant</code> if it is from 20 to 30 (both included),</li><li><code>Cold</code> if it is less than 20.</li></ul>\n<p>Fix the error.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ntemp = int(input())\nif temp > 30:\n    print(\"Hot\")\nelse if temp >= 20:\n    print(\"Pleasant\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Cold\")\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
