    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "L06_007_Missing Comma",
   "display_name": "Missing Comma",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Items in a list, and arguments to a function such as <code>print</code>, are separated by <b>commas</b>. If a comma is missing, Python sees two things side by side with nothing joining them, which is not allowed:</p>\n<pre>colours = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot; &quot;blue&quot;]\nprint(&quot;Colours:&quot; colours)</pre>\n<p>The second line gives:</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  print(&quot;Colours:&quot; colours)\n        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nSyntaxError: invalid syntax. Perhaps you forgot a comma?</pre>\n<p>(The first line is also a mistake, but a sneaky one: Python joins two strings written next to each other, so the list silently becomes <code>[\"red\", \"greenblue\"]</code>. Mistakes that give no error message are called <b>logical errors</b>; you will meet many of them at the end of this lab.) (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads two integers (one per line) and should print <code>Sum:</code> followed by their sum, separated by a space. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the inputs are <code>3</code> and <code>4</code>, the output is <code>Sum: 7</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\na = int(input())\nb = int(input())\nprint(\"Sum:\" a + b)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "3\n4",
     "expected": "Sum: 7",
     "extra": "",
