  {
   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "L06_008_Expected An Indented Block",
   "display_name": "Expected An Indented Block",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python uses <b>indentation</b> (spaces at the start of a line) to know which lines belong inside a block. After a line ending in <code>:</code> (an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or <code>def</code>), the next line <b>must</b> be indented. If it is not, Python reports an <code>IndentationError</code>:</p>\n<pre>for k in range(2):\nprint(k)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  print(k)\n  ^\nIndentationError: expected an indented block after &#x27;for&#x27; statement on line 1</pre>\n<p><code>IndentationError</code> is a special kind of syntax error: Python cannot even start running the program. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a positive integer <code>n</code> (int &gt; 0) and should print the squares <code>1*1, 2*2, ..., n*n</code>, one per line. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>3</code>, the output is:</p>\n<pre>1\n4\n9</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\nprint(i * i)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "3",
     "expected": "1\n4\n9",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "1",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "1\n4\n9\n16\n25",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "4",
     "expected": "1\n4\n9\n16",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L06_009_Unexpected Indent",
   "display_name": "Unexpected Indent",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The opposite mistake: a line is indented when it should not be. Only lines inside a block (after a line ending with <code>:</code>) may be indented. A stray indent, often from a stray Tab or from copying code, gives:</p>\n<pre>x = 10\n    y = 20\nprint(x + y)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  y = 20\nIndentationError: unexpected indent</pre>\n<p>Lines that are not inside any block must start at the very left edge, with no spaces in front. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads two integers (one per line) and should print their product. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the inputs are <code>3</code> and <code>4</code>, the output is <code>12</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\na = int(input())\n    b = int(input())\nprint(a * b)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "3\n4",
     "expected": "12",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0\n9",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-2\n6",
     "expected": "-12",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "7\n7",
     "expected": "49",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L06_010_Unindent Does Not Match",
   "display_name": "Unindent Does Not Match",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>When a block ends, the next line must go back to <b>exactly</b> the indentation of an earlier line. Going back only part of the way (say 2 spaces instead of 0 or 4) confuses Python:</p>\n<pre>if 5 &gt; 3:\n    print(&quot;yes&quot;)\n  print(&quot;done&quot;)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 3\n  print(&quot;done&quot;)\n               ^\nIndentationError: unindent does not match any outer indentation level</pre>\n<p>The fix depends on what the line should do: if it belongs <b>inside</b> the block, indent it like the other lines of the block; if it should run <b>after</b> the block, move it all the way back. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads an integer and should print <code>Positive</code> if it is more than 0, otherwise <code>Not positive</code>. After that, it should always print <code>Done</code> (for every input). Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>5</code>, the output is:</p>\n<pre>Positive\nDone</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\nif n > 0:\n    print(\"Positive\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not positive\")\n  print(\"Done\")\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "Positive\nDone",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "Not positive\nDone",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-4",
     "expected": "Not positive\nDone",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "12",
     "expected": "Positive\nDone",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L06_011_Body Of Def Not Indented",
   "display_name": "Body Of Def Not Indented",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The body of a function is a block too, so every line of it must be indented under the <code>def</code> line:</p>\n<pre>def greet():\nprint(&quot;Hi&quot;)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2\n  print(&quot;Hi&quot;)\n  ^\nIndentationError: expected an indented block after function definition on line 1</pre>\n<p>From now on most questions are <b>functions</b>, as in Labs 4 and 5: the tests call your function, for example <code>print(cube(3))</code>, and compare what is printed. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>cube(x)</code>, which should return <code>x * x * x</code> for any integer <code>x</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>cube(3)</code> returns <code>27</code> and <code>cube(-2)</code> returns <code>-8</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef cube(x):\nreturn x * x * x\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "27",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
