     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(-2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-8",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(5))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "125",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(-4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-64",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(cube(10))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1000",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L06_012_Misspelled Variable",
   "display_name": "Misspelled Variable",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Now we move from syntax errors to <b>runtime errors</b>. The program starts running, and stops at the line where something goes wrong. The message shows the line number, the error type and a short explanation.</p>\n<p>A <code>NameError</code> means Python met a name (a variable or a function) that it does not know. The most common cause is a <b>spelling mistake</b>: a variable is created with one spelling and used with another.</p>\n<pre>speed = 60\ntime = 2\ndistance = sped * time</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 3, in &lt;module&gt;\n  distance = sped * time\nNameError: name &#x27;sped&#x27; is not defined. Did you mean: &#x27;speed&#x27;?</pre>\n<p>Newer versions of Python even suggest the name you probably meant (\"Did you mean: ...\"). (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>final_price(price, discount)</code>. It is given a price and a discount percentage (from 0 to 100) and should return the price after the discount, rounded to 2 decimal places with <code>round(value, 2)</code>. For example, <code>print(round(3.14159, 2))</code> gives <code>3.14</code>.</p>\n<p>The discount amount is <code>price * discount / 100</code>. For example, <code>final_price(200, 10)</code> returns <code>180.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef final_price(price, discount):\n    discount_amount = price * discount / 100\n    return round(price - discount_amont, 2)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(final_price(200, 10))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "180.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(final_price(50, 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "50.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(final_price(99.5, 20))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "79.6",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(final_price(1000, 15))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "850.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 13,
   "xml_name": "L06_013_Wrong Capital Letters",
   "display_name": "Wrong Capital Letters",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python is <b>case-sensitive</b>: <code>total</code>, <code>Total</code> and <code>TOTAL</code> are three different names. The same is true for built-in functions: <code>print</code> works, but <code>Print</code> does not exist.</p>\n<pre>Count = 3\nprint(count)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(count)\nNameError: name &#x27;count&#x27; is not defined. Did you mean: &#x27;Count&#x27;?</pre>\n<p>A good habit: write all your variable names in lowercase, with <code>_</code> between words (<code>max_value</code>, <code>total_marks</code>). (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>rectangle_area(length, width)</code>, which should return the area of a rectangle, <code>length * width</code> (both are non-negative integers).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>rectangle_area(3, 4)</code> returns <code>12</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef rectangle_area(length, width):\n    Area = length * width\n    return area\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(rectangle_area(3, 4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "12",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(rectangle_area(5, 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(rectangle_area(7, 8))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "56",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(rectangle_area(1, 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 14,
   "xml_name": "L06_014_Text Without Quotes",
   "display_name": "Text Without Quotes",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Text that should be printed as it is must be inside quotes. Without quotes, Python thinks the word is the <b>name</b> of a variable, and if there is no such variable you get a <code>NameError</code>:</p>\n<pre>print(Welcome)</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(Welcome)\nNameError: name &#x27;Welcome&#x27; is not defined</pre>\n<p>Programs often have the <b>same mistake in more than one place</b>. Python stops at the first error, so after you fix one, Pre-check again: there may be another one further down. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a name and should print two lines: <code>Welcome</code> followed by the name (separated by a space), and then <code>Goodbye</code>. Fix all the errors.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>Asha</code>, the output is:</p>\n<pre>Welcome Asha\nGoodbye</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nname = input()\nprint(Welcome, name)\nprint(Goodbye)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Asha",
     "expected": "Welcome Asha\nGoodbye",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Luffy",
     "expected": "Welcome Luffy\nGoodbye",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Ravi Kumar",
     "expected": "Welcome Ravi Kumar\nGoodbye",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "Frodo",
     "expected": "Welcome Frodo\nGoodbye",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L06_015_Called Before Definition",
   "display_name": "Called Before Definition",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python runs a program <b>from top to bottom</b>. A <code>def</code> line creates the function only when Python reaches it. So if you call a function on a line <b>above</b> its <code>def</code>, the function does not exist yet:</p>\n<pre>print(half(10))\n\ndef half(x):\n    return x / 2</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(half(10))\nNameError: name &#x27;half&#x27; is not defined</pre>\n<p>Always put your function definitions first, and the code that uses them after. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads an integer <code>n</code> and should print <code>2 * n</code> using the function <code>double_it</code>. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>4</code>, the output is <code>8</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nn = int(input())\nprint(double_it(n))\n\ndef double_it(x):\n    return 2 * x\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "4",
     "expected": "8",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-3",
     "expected": "-6",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "50",
     "expected": "100",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 16,
   "xml_name": "L06_016_Input Not Converted",
   "display_name": "Input Not Converted",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Every value in Python has a <b>type</b>: <code>int</code> (whole numbers like <code>7</code>), <code>float</code> (decimals like <code>2.5</code>), <code>str</code> (text like <code>\"7\"</code>), <code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>), <code>list</code>, and so on. A <code>TypeError</code> means an operation was used on the wrong type of value.</p>\n<p>A very common case: <code>input()</code> always gives <b>text</b> (a <code>str</code>), even when the user types digits. Text and a number cannot be added:</p>\n<pre>year = input()\nprint(year + 1)</pre>\n<p>If the user types <code>2025</code>, this gives:</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(year + 1)\nTypeError: can only concatenate str (not &quot;int&quot;) to str</pre>\n<p>The fix is to convert the text to a number with <code>int(...)</code> (or <code>float(...)</code> for decimals) as soon as you read it. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads a person's age (a non-negative integer) and should print <code>Next year you will be</code> followed by the age next year. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the input is <code>18</code>, the output is <code>Next year you will be 19</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nage = input()\nnext_age = age + 1\nprint(\"Next year you will be\", next_age)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "18",
     "expected": "Next year you will be 19",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "Next year you will be 1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "45",
     "expected": "Next year you will be 46",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "7",
     "expected": "Next year you will be 8",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 17,
   "xml_name": "L06_017_Adding Text And A Number",
   "display_name": "Adding Text And A Number",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <code>+</code> operator means <b>add</b> for numbers but <b>join</b> for strings. Python does not guess what you want when one side is text and the other is a number:</p>\n<pre>score = 42\nmessage = &quot;Score: &quot; + score</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  message = &quot;Score: &quot; + score\nTypeError: can only concatenate str (not &quot;int&quot;) to str</pre>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>str(x)</code> converts a value to text, so <code>\"Score: \" + str(score)</code> gives the string <code>\"Score: 42\"</code>. (Inside <code>print</code> you can also use commas, <code>print(\"Score:\", score)</code>, but a function that must <b>return</b> one string needs <code>str()</code>.) (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>bill_message(price, quantity)</code> (both non-negative integers). It should return the text <code>Total: </code> followed by <code>price * quantity</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>bill_message(20, 3)</code> returns <code>\"Total: 60\"</code>, so <code>print(bill_message(20, 3))</code> prints <code>Total: 60</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef bill_message(price, quantity):\n    total = price * quantity\n    return \"Total: \" + total\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(bill_message(20, 3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Total: 60",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(bill_message(5, 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Total: 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(bill_message(99, 2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Total: 198",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(bill_message(1, 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Total: 1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 18,
   "xml_name": "L06_018_Wrong Number Of Arguments",
   "display_name": "Wrong Number Of Arguments",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>When you call a function, you must give it <b>exactly as many arguments as its <code>def</code> line has parameters</b>. Too few or too many gives a <code>TypeError</code> that names the function and the missing argument:</p>\n<pre>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(5))</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 4, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(add(5))\nTypeError: add() missing 1 required positional argument: &#x27;b&#x27;</pre>\n<p>Notice that this traceback has <b>more than one</b> <code>File ... line ...</code> entry: it lists the chain of calls. The last entry is where the error happened; the ones above show which line called it. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the code below. <code>rectangle_perimeter(length, width)</code> returns <code>2 * (length + width)</code> and is correct. <code>square_perimeter(side)</code> should return the perimeter of a square with that side (a square is a rectangle whose length and width are equal), but it has an error. All sides are non-negative integers.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>square_perimeter(5)</code> returns <code>20</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef rectangle_perimeter(length, width):\n    return 2 * (length + width)\n\ndef square_perimeter(side):\n    return rectangle_perimeter(side)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(square_perimeter(5))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "20",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(square_perimeter(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(rectangle_perimeter(2, 3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "10",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(square_perimeter(7))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "28",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(square_perimeter(13))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "52",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "L06_019_Length Of A Number",
   "display_name": "Length Of A Number",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>len()</code> gives the length of a <b>string</b> or a <b>list</b>: <code>len(\"cat\")</code> is 3 and <code>len([4, 8, 1])</code> is 3. A number such as <code>345</code> has no length, so <code>len(345)</code> is a <code>TypeError</code>:</p>\n<pre>n = 2025\nprint(len(n))</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(len(n))\nTypeError: object of type &#x27;int&#x27; has no len()</pre>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>str(n)</code> turns a number into text: <code>str(2025)</code> is <code>\"2025\"</code>, and <code>len(str(2025))</code> is 4. (You could also count digits with a <code>while</code> loop and <code>// 10</code>, as in Lab 4.) (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>count_digits(n)</code>, which should return the number of digits of a non-negative integer <code>n</code> (int &gt;= 0). The number <code>0</code> has 1 digit.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>count_digits(345)</code> returns <code>3</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef count_digits(n):\n    return len(n)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_digits(345))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_digits(7))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_digits(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_digits(100000))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "6",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
