     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L06_020_Print Instead Of Return",
   "display_name": "Print Instead Of Return",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>print</code> and <code>return</code> are not the same:</p>\n<ul><li><code>print(x)</code> <b>shows</b> <code>x</code> on the screen. The value is gone after that; the caller cannot use it.</li><li><code>return x</code> <b>gives</b> <code>x</code> back to the caller, who can store it or calculate with it.</li></ul>\n<p>A function with no <code>return</code> gives back a special value, <b><code>None</code></b> (meaning \"nothing\"). Calculating with <code>None</code> gives a <code>TypeError</code>:</p>\n<pre>def triple(x):\n    print(3 * x)\n\nresult = triple(4) + 1</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 4, in &lt;module&gt;\n  result = triple(4) + 1\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: &#x27;NoneType&#x27; and &#x27;int&#x27;</pre>\n<p>Here <code>12</code> is printed first, and then the program stops. <code>NoneType</code> is the type of <code>None</code>. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the code below so that <code>sum_of_squares(a, b)</code> returns <code>a * a + b * b</code> (for any integers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>), using the helper function <code>square</code>. Nothing else should be printed.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>sum_of_squares(3, 4)</code> returns <code>25</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef square(x):\n    print(x * x)\n\ndef sum_of_squares(a, b):\n    return square(a) + square(b)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(sum_of_squares(3, 4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "25",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(sum_of_squares(0, 2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(sum_of_squares(-1, 6))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "37",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(sum_of_squares(10, 10))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "200",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 21,
   "xml_name": "L06_021_Decimal Input With Int",
   "display_name": "Decimal Input With Int",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <code>ValueError</code> means the <b>type</b> is right but the <b>value</b> is not acceptable. For example, <code>int()</code> accepts text, but only text that looks like a whole number: <code>int(\"42\")</code> works, but <code>int(\"4.5\")</code> and <code>int(\"hello\")</code> do not:</p>\n<pre>weight = int(input())</pre>\n<p>If the user types <code>62.5</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n  weight = int(input())\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: &#x27;62.5&#x27;</pre>\n<p>For inputs that may have a decimal point, use <code>float(...)</code> instead of <code>int(...)</code>. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the program below reads the price of one item (a non-negative number, possibly with decimals) and then a quantity (a non-negative integer), and should print <code>Total:</code> followed by <code>price * quantity</code> rounded to 2 decimal places with <code>round(value, 2)</code>. Fix the error.</p>\n<p>For example, if the inputs are <code>12.5</code> and <code>4</code>, the output is <code>Total: 50.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\nprice = int(input())\nqty = int(input())\nprint(\"Total:\", round(price * qty, 2))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "12.5\n4",
     "expected": "Total: 50.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "9.99\n3",
     "expected": "Total: 29.97",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "100\n2",
     "expected": "Total: 200.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0.5\n7",
     "expected": "Total: 3.5",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "149.95\n2",
     "expected": "Total: 299.9",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 22,
   "xml_name": "L06_022_Index Of A Missing Value",
   "display_name": "Index Of A Missing Value",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Lab 5 you met <code>lst.index(x)</code>, which gives the position of the first <code>x</code> in a list: <code>[4, 7, 9].index(7)</code> is <code>1</code>. But if <code>x</code> is <b>not</b> in the list, <code>index</code> has no answer to give, and raises a <code>ValueError</code>:</p>\n<pre>days = [&quot;Mon&quot;, &quot;Tue&quot;, &quot;Wed&quot;]\nprint(days.index(&quot;Sun&quot;))</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(days.index(&quot;Sun&quot;))\nValueError: &#x27;Sun&#x27; is not in list</pre>\n<p>To avoid this, <b>check first</b> with the <code>in</code> operator (<code>x in lst</code> is <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>), and use <code>index</code> only when the value is there. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>position_of(lst, x)</code>. It should return the position (index) of the first <code>x</code> in the list of integers <code>lst</code>, or <code>-1</code> if <code>x</code> is not in the list (this includes an empty list).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>position_of([4, 7, 9], 7)</code> returns <code>1</code> and <code>position_of([4, 7, 9], 5)</code> returns <code>-1</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef position_of(lst, x):\n    return lst.index(x)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(position_of([4, 7, 9], 5))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(position_of([4, 7, 9], 7))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(position_of([], 3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(position_of([2, 8, 2], 2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(position_of(lst, 6))",
     "stdin": "1 3 5 7",
     "expected": "-1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(position_of(lst, 13))",
     "stdin": "10 11 12 13 14",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 23,
   "xml_name": "L06_023_Loop One Step Too Far",
   "display_name": "Loop One Step Too Far",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The positions (indices) of a list with <code>n</code> items are <code>0, 1, ..., n - 1</code>. There is <b>no</b> position <code>n</code>. Asking for a position that does not exist gives an <code>IndexError</code>:</p>\n<pre>nums = [10, 20, 30]\nprint(nums[3])</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(nums[3])\nIndexError: list index out of range</pre>\n<p>A loop like <code>for i in range(len(lst)):</code> visits exactly the valid positions <code>0</code> to <code>len(lst) - 1</code>, because <code>range(k)</code> stops <b>before</b> <code>k</code>. Adding <code>+ 1</code> makes the loop go one step too far. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>list_sum(lst)</code>, which should return the sum of all the integers in <code>lst</code>. The sum of an empty list is <code>0</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>list_sum([4, 8, 15])</code> returns <code>27</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef list_sum(lst):\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(len(lst) + 1):\n        total = total + lst[i]\n    return total\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_sum([4, 8, 15]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "27",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_sum([5]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "5",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(list_sum([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(list_sum(lst))",
     "stdin": "3 -1 20 7",
     "expected": "29",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(list_sum(lst))",
     "stdin": "100 200 300 400 500",
     "expected": "1500",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 24,
   "xml_name": "L06_024_Last Element Of A List",
   "display_name": "Last Element Of A List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To get the <b>last</b> item of a list, remember that positions start at 0, so the last position is <code>len(lst) - 1</code>, not <code>len(lst)</code>:</p>\n<pre>letters = [&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;]\nprint(letters[len(letters)])</pre>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">File &quot;__tester__.python3&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n  print(letters[len(letters)])\nIndexError: list index out of range</pre>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> negative positions count from the end: <code>lst[-1]</code> is the last item, <code>lst[-2]</code> the one before it. So <code>lst[-1]</code> and <code>lst[len(lst) - 1]</code> are the same item. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>first_plus_last(lst)</code>, which should return the first item plus the last item of a <b>non-empty</b> list of integers. (For a list with one item, that item is both first and last.)</p>\n<p>For example, <code>first_plus_last([3, 9, 4])</code> returns <code>7</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef first_plus_last(lst):\n    return lst[0] + lst[len(lst)]\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(first_plus_last([3, 9, 4]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "7",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(first_plus_last([6]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "12",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(first_plus_last(lst))",
     "stdin": "10 -5 8 2 -3",
     "expected": "7",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(first_plus_last(lst))",
