  {
   "xml_index": 25,
   "xml_name": "L06_025_While Loop Past The End",
   "display_name": "While Loop Past The End",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The same off-by-one mistake happens with <code>while</code> loops. To visit every position of a list, the loop must <b>stop before</b> <code>len(lst)</code>, so the condition is <code>i &lt; len(lst)</code>, not <code>i &lt;= len(lst)</code>.</p>\n<p>A good way to check a loop bound: take a tiny list, say <code>[7, 8]</code> (positions 0 and 1), and write down the value of <code>i</code> each time the condition is tested: <code>0</code> (yes), <code>1</code> (yes), <code>2</code> (must be no!).</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>count_even(lst)</code>, which should return how many integers in <code>lst</code> are even. An empty list has 0 even numbers. (Negative numbers can be even too: <code>-4 % 2</code> is <code>0</code>.)</p>\n<p>For example, <code>count_even([1, 2, 3, 4])</code> returns <code>2</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef count_even(lst):\n    count = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i <= len(lst):\n        if lst[i] % 2 == 0:\n            count = count + 1\n        i = i + 1\n    return count\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
