  {
   "xml_index": 26,
   "xml_name": "L06_026_Empty List Has No First Item",
   "display_name": "Empty List Has No First Item",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>An <b>empty list</b> <code>[]</code> has no items at all, so even <code>lst[0]</code> is an <code>IndexError</code>. Code that starts with the first item, like \"the biggest so far is <code>lst[0]</code>\", must handle the empty list separately, <b>before</b> touching <code>lst[0]</code>:</p>\n<pre>if len(lst) == 0:\n    return ...   # the answer for an empty list</pre>\n<p>When a problem says what to do for an empty list, that is a hint that the empty list will be tested. (The exact wording of error messages can differ a little between Python versions, but the error type and the line number are what matter.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>largest(lst)</code>, which should return the largest integer in <code>lst</code>, or <code>0</code> if the list is empty.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>largest([3, 9, 2])</code> returns <code>9</code> and <code>largest([])</code> returns <code>0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code has at least one error. Use Pre-check (free) to see the error message.\n# Fix the code, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef largest(lst):\n    biggest = lst[0]\n    for x in lst:\n        if x > biggest:\n            biggest = x\n    return biggest\n",
