  {
   "xml_index": 38,
   "xml_name": "L06_038_Division Operators Mixed Up",
   "display_name": "Division Operators Mixed Up",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python has three division operators, and mixing them up is a common logical error:</p>\n<ul><li><code>/</code> gives the exact answer, always a float: <code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3.5</code>.</li><li><code>//</code> gives the whole number of times it fits (integer division): <code>7 // 2</code> is <code>3</code>.</li><li><code>%</code> gives the <b>remainder</b>: <code>7 % 2</code> is <code>1</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, 130 seconds is <code>130 // 60 = 2</code> minutes and <code>130 % 60 = 10</code> seconds.</p>\n<p>This function returns <b>two values</b> (as in Lab 4), and the tests use them like this:</p>\n<pre>h, m = split_minutes(125)\nprint(h, m)</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>split_minutes(total_minutes)</code>. For a non-negative integer <code>total_minutes</code> (int &gt;= 0), it should return the number of whole hours and the minutes left over.</p>\n<p>For example, 125 minutes is 2 hours and 5 minutes, so the test above prints <code>2 5</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code runs, but gives wrong answers for some valid inputs.\n# Find and fix the bug, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef split_minutes(total_minutes):\n    hours = total_minutes / 60\n    minutes = total_minutes // 60\n    return hours, minutes\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
