  {
   "xml_index": 40,
   "xml_name": "L06_040_Wrong Starting Value",
   "display_name": "Wrong Starting Value",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>starting value</b> of a variable matters. To find the largest item, a common mistake is to start with <code>biggest = 0</code>. This works when some item is more than 0, but if <b>every</b> item is negative, no item beats 0, and the function wrongly returns 0.</p>\n<p><b>Test corner cases:</b> a program that works on \"normal\" inputs may fail on unusual ones: all negative numbers, zeros, a list with one item, equal items. When you test, always try a few of these.</p>\n<p>The safe start is the <b>first item of the list</b>, <code>lst[0]</code>: the largest item is at least that big.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>max_temperature(temps)</code>, which should return the highest temperature in a <b>non-empty</b> list of integer temperatures (which can be negative, for example in winter in Ladakh).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>max_temperature([-5, -2, -9])</code> returns <code>-2</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code runs, but gives wrong answers for some valid inputs.\n# Find and fix the bug, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef max_temperature(temps):\n    biggest = 0\n    for t in temps:\n        if t > biggest:\n            biggest = t\n    return biggest\n",
