  {
   "xml_index": 45,
   "xml_name": "L06_045_Return Inside The Loop",
   "display_name": "Return Inside The Loop",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>From here on, the questions work like the debugging questions in exams: the code <b>passes the example test</b>, so Pre-check alone will not show the bug. The bug appears only for some other inputs, which are checked by hidden tests. Read the code carefully, think of <b>corner cases</b> (boundaries, the first and last items, negative numbers, odd and even values) and test them yourself by adding a <code>print(...)</code> call below the function (remove it before Check).</p>\n<p>Indentation decides <b>where a line belongs</b>. A <code>return</code> (or <code>print</code>) that is indented one level too deep ends up <b>inside</b> the loop, and runs during the first step instead of after the loop. Python gives no error, because both places are valid; only the answer is wrong.</p>\n<p>When you read a loop, check each line: should it run <b>once per item</b> (inside the loop) or <b>once at the end</b> (after the loop, at the same indentation as the <code>for</code>)?</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>count_multiples(lst, k)</code>, which should return how many integers in <code>lst</code> are multiples of <code>k</code> (divisible by <code>k</code>, i.e. <code>x % k == 0</code>). Inputs your solution must work on: a non-empty list of integers and a positive integer <code>k</code> (int &gt; 0).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>count_multiples([4, 5, 6], 4)</code> returns <code>1</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code runs, but gives wrong answers for some valid inputs.\n# Find and fix the bug, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef count_multiples(lst, k):\n    count = 0\n    for x in lst:\n        if x % k == 0:\n            count = count + 1\n        return count\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_multiples([4, 5, 6], 4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(count_multiples(lst, 3))",
     "stdin": "3 6 9",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(count_multiples(lst, 2))",
     "stdin": "1 2 4 8",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "lst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(count_multiples(lst, 5))",
     "stdin": "5 10 15 7",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 46,
   "xml_name": "L06_046_Recursion With Wrong Step",
   "display_name": "Recursion With Wrong Step",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>From here on, the questions work like the debugging questions in exams: the code <b>passes the example test</b>, so Pre-check alone will not show the bug. The bug appears only for some other inputs, which are checked by hidden tests. Read the code carefully, think of <b>corner cases</b> (boundaries, the first and last items, negative numbers, odd and even values) and test them yourself by adding a <code>print(...)</code> call below the function (remove it before Check).</p>\n<p>In a recursive function, check two things: the <b>base case</b> (is the answer right for the smallest inputs?) and the <b>recursive step</b> (does it split the problem correctly?).</p>\n<p>For digits, recall: <code>n % 10</code> is the <b>last digit</b> of <code>n</code>, and <code>n // 10</code> is <code>n</code> <b>without</b> its last digit. For example, for <code>n = 347</code>: <code>n % 10</code> is <code>7</code> and <code>n // 10</code> is <code>34</code>. So \"sum of digits of 347\" = <code>7</code> + \"sum of digits of 34\".</p>\n<p>Bugs in the recursive step often hide in short inputs: a mistake may give the right answer for every 2-digit number and only show up with 3 or more digits. Trace the calls for a longer input by hand.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the recursive function <code>digit_sum(n)</code>, which should return the sum of the digits of a non-negative integer <code>n</code> (int &gt;= 0).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>digit_sum(47)</code> returns <code>11</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# This code runs, but gives wrong answers for some valid inputs.\n# Find and fix the bug, and add a comment saying what you changed.\ndef digit_sum(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return n\n    return n // 10 + digit_sum(n % 10)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(digit_sum(47))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "11",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(digit_sum(123))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "6",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
